  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies remain mostly clear this morning\nacross northern and central Nevada. The upper pattern has remained\nfairly consistent over the past several days as a strong upper\nlevel ridge sits across Arizona and New Mexico with an upper\ntrough across the Pacific Northwest. For today, sufficient\nmoisture and stability will be across the eastern portions of the\nforecast area such that there is a ten to twenty percent\nprobability for thunderstorm formation during the afternoon. Main\nimpacts will be cloud to ground lightning strikes and outflow\nwinds to 45 mph. Highs today will be in the 90s with lows tonight\nin the 50s. Look for this pattern to continue into tomorrow as \nthe aforementioned high pressure system retrogrades slightly to\nthe west. Beginning Friday, models continue to depict a\ndisturbance in the flow that will cross California into Nevada,\nbringing a resultant southwest flow to the area along with\nsubtropical moisture into the forecast area. Uncertainty regarding\nhow deep this moisture is continues to be low to moderate but the\ncurrent forecast would bring shower and thunderstorm chances to \nmuch of the forecast area from Friday into the weekend. Moisture \nis currently anticipated to linger into early next week, with \nweaker and more isolated storms Sunday and Monday. Afternoon high\ntemperatures will begin a downward shift to more normal levels on\nFriday with readings in the 80s and lows in the 50s.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Changes were made to PoPs and\nweather for the short term, introducing thunderstorms to the east.\nConfidence is low to moderate in storm chances and associated \nimpacts through tomorrow. Confidence remains high with respect to in\nincreasing moisture and atmospheric instability for Friday and \nSaturday but low confidence in moisture levels and extent of \nimpacts.",
